# deploy.conf — values specific to THIS host (the only place one deployment differs from another).
#
# Copy to deploy/deploy.conf (gitignored, like the env file in /etc) and fill in for the machine you
# are deploying to, then run deploy/render-config.sh to generate its systemd units and Caddy site
# file from the committed templates. Nothing host-specific is baked into a tracked file, so moving
# to another machine is: edit this, re-render.
#
# The values below are illustrative defaults, not a live deployment. Change what you need to.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO ACCOUNTS, because there are two jobs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# DEPLOY identity. Owns the checkout, runs git pull / npm ci / npm run build, and needs sudo —
# installing systemd units and reloading Caddy is root's work. This is the account you log into to
# operate the service, so give it a shell and its own SSH key.
#
# It also needs a real password: sudo authenticates against the password field, so an account
# created with --disabled-password ends up in the sudo group and still unable to sudo.
SERVICE_USER=zfin-api-admin

# RUNTIME identity. What the API and loader processes actually run as. A system account: no
# password, no shell, no home, never logged into. It needs exactly four things — read the checkout,
# read the deployment env file, listen on APP_PORT, reach the database — and a flaw in the Node
# process yields those and nothing else.
#
# Keep this SEPARATE from the deploy account. If one account does both, the service runs as a member
# of the sudo group, and anyone who compromises the process can wait for a password rather than
# needing one.
#
# One runtime account per service, rather than one shared by every service on the machine: a
# compromise should reach one service's credentials, not all of them.
RUNTIME_USER=zfin-api

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The code checkout root — also the systemd WorkingDirectory for both units.
#
# NOT a home directory. The runtime account has to read this tree, and a home directory is 0750 to
# its owner, so the service could not traverse into it. The units additionally set ProtectHome=yes,
# which hides /home from them altogether.
#
# Owned by the deploy user, group-readable by the runtime user, setgid so that a later `npm ci`
# keeps the group on everything it writes:
#     sudo install -d -o $SERVICE_USER -g $RUNTIME_USER -m 2750 /srv/zfin-data-api
APP_DIR=/srv/zfin-data-api

# Absolute path to the Node binary (systemd has no nvm or PATH). Node 20+ is required by NestJS 11.
#
# A SYSTEM Node, not one under a user's ~/.nvm. Same reason as APP_DIR: the runtime account cannot
# read another user's home, and ProtectHome=yes hides /home from the unit besides. Installing it
# system-wide also means the version that builds the code is the version that runs it.
NODE_BIN=/usr/bin/node

# The public hostname Caddy serves and obtains a Let's Encrypt certificate for. DNS must already
# resolve this name to this machine before the Caddy step, or the certificate cannot be issued.
#
# REPLACING AN EXISTING DEPLOYMENT? Put a STAGING name here first — the live name still resolves to
# the old machine, and pointing this at it before cutover only has Caddy chase a certificate for a
# host that answers somewhere else. Verify the new deployment against the old one, then re-render
# with the real name once DNS moves.
SITE_ADDRESS=zfin-api.example.org

# The localhost port the API listens on. Must match PORT in the deployment env file;
# render-config.sh refuses to render if the two disagree. Check what is free:  ss -ltn
APP_PORT=3480
